import java.util.*;

// Definition of singly linked list
class ListNode {

    // Stores the value of the current node
    public int data;

    // Points to the next node
    public ListNode next;

    // Default constructor
    ListNode() {
        data = 0;
        next = null;
    }

    // Constructor to create a node with data
    ListNode(int x) {
        data = x;
        next = null;
    }

    // Constructor to create a node with data
    // and connect it to the next node
    ListNode(int x, ListNode next) {
        data = x;
        this.next = next;
    }
}


class Solution {

    public ListNode deleteTail(ListNode head) {

        // Case 1: If the list is empty
        if (head == null) {
            return null;
        }

        // Case 2: If there is only one node
        // After deleting it, the list becomes empty
        if (head.next == null) {
            return null;
        }

        // Start travelling from the first node
        ListNode current = head;

        /*
            Example:

            head
             ↓
            [7] → [8] → [10] → null

            current starts at 7
        */

        // Travel until current reaches the second-last node
        while (current.next.next != null) {

            // Move current to the next node
            current = current.next;
        }

        /*
            For:

            [7] → [8] → [10] → null

            current finally becomes:

                   current
                      ↓
            [7] → [8] → [10] → null

            Here current = 8

            current.next = 10 (the tail)
        */

        // Remove the last node
        // The second-last node now points to null
        current.next = null;

        /*
            After deletion:

            head
             ↓
            [7] → [8] → null

            The node [10] is no longer part
            of the linked list.
        */

        // Return the original head
        return head;
    }
}

Delete Tail of Linked List — Revision

To delete the last node of a singly linked list, first handle the edge cases: if the list is empty or has only one node, return null. Otherwise, start a current pointer at head and move it until it reaches the second-last node using current.next.next != null. Then set current.next = null to remove the last node. Finally, return head, because the first node of the remaining linked list is still the same.

Time: O(n) — we may traverse the entire list.
Auxiliary Space: O(1) — only one pointer current is used.
